# Documentation lifecycle

`docs/README.md` is the front door. This rule is how documents are
classified, closed, and kept from regenerating the 15k-line FOLLOWUPS /
26k-line CHANGELOG failure mode.

Cite from [`91-documentation-after-plans.mdc`](91-documentation-after-plans.mdc)
and [`94-tracking-index.mdc`](94-tracking-index.mdc). Archive eligibility
and the `git mv` checklist live in
[`docs/design/IMPLEMENTATION_INDEX.md`](../../docs/design/IMPLEMENTATION_INDEX.md)
§8; this rule does not restate that checklist.

## Document classes

| Class | Lives in | Banner |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Contract of record | `docs/` or `docs/design/` forever | `Status: LIVING CONTRACT` + last-verified date |
| Active plan | `docs/design/` | `Status: OPEN` + round |
| Closed plan / preflight / retired concept | `docs/completed/` | `Status: CLOSED-as-record` or `RETIRED` |
| Operator / user | `docs/` root | no round archaeology |
| Ledger | FOLLOWUPS, CHANGELOG, decision log | not a work queue |
| Map | `IMPLEMENTATION_INDEX.md` | identifier + owning doc + one-line status |

Do **not** implement from `docs/completed/`. Git history is the archive.

Claim-era staking specs (confidential / lock-tier / `txin_stake_claim*`)
are deleted, not stubbed. Archival staking SoTs (`V3_STAKER_ARCHIVAL.md`,
`PHASE_2B_FSM_RETOOL.md`, `PRINCIPAL_STAKE_LIFECYCLE.md`,
`REWARD_EMISSION_*`, `ARCHIVAL_*.md`, `EMISSION_CLAIM_BUILDER.md`) are
never deleted as a side-effect of that class.

## Status banner

Every file under `docs/design/` and `docs/completed/` carries a
**Status** line in the first 40 lines after the H1. The banner is the
first thing a grep-driven reader sees.

## Completing a plan includes archive-or-contract

When the work a plan doc owns is done:

- If it is a living contract of record, it **stays** in `docs/design/`
  (or `docs/`) and the banner flips to `LIVING CONTRACT` with a
  last-verified date.
- If it is a plan / preflight / closeout / retired concept, `git mv` it
  to `docs/completed/` in the **same** change, following index §8.

“Implementation landed” alone is not archival. Mixed docs that still
own named open residue stay in `design/`.

## FOLLOWUPS is a queue of one-liners

Each item is **≤20 lines**: one sentence of what remains, a `Target:`
from the three tokens below, and a link to the owning doc. Essays belong
in the plan doc or the completed record, not in the queue.

Allowed `Target:` values only:

- **pre-genesis** — default. Lands before genesis if it should exist at
  launch.
- **post-genesis** — exceptional deferral with a **named blocker** why
  it cannot land before genesis. The list stays tiny.
- **V4** — lattice-only transition, 2–5 years, gated on NIST (or
  successor) actually approving primitives such as lattice threshold
  signatures. Not a parking lot.

`V3.1` / `V3.2` / `V3.x` / `V5+` are not work-item targets. `V3.1` may
remain in a historical protocol *title*.

Resolved items are **removed**. Git history is the archive.

## CHANGELOG scope

`docs/CHANGELOG.md` records user-visible, consensus, API, or
security-relevant deltas. Design-round archaeology stays in the plan
doc. A plan that only rearranges documentation does not owe a
CHANGELOG novel; a one-line Unreleased note is enough when the change
is process-only.

CI (`.github/workflows/doc-links.yml`) holds the banners, FOLLOWUPS
`Target:` tokens, forbidden fossils in always-applied rules, index §2
prefix uniqueness, and the claim-era basename refusal. A closed plan
left in `docs/design/` with a DRAFT banner, or a new
`CONFIDENTIAL_STAKING.md`, fails the run.

## Who wins

Code on `dev` > living contract > implementation index (map) >
plan-doc status table > FOLLOWUPS. Fix the loser; do not
drive-by-update every mention.

